WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.000
On one day every year in the city of London you can encounter some extraordinary wildlife.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:19.520
Hundreds of gorillas are roaming the streets.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:25.240
You wouldn't catch me running in one of these gorilla suits but nonetheless the great gorilla

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:37.960
run does raise great sums of money to help protect mountain gorillas in Central Africa.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:45.520
Wow!

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:53.280
I met the real mountain gorillas over a quarter of a century ago in Africa and it's something

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:56.320
I shall never ever forget.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:01.840
At the time they were on the verge of extinction but since then their numbers have increased

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:07.040
despite all of's and they've become a conservation success story.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:11.800
How is it that mountain gorillas have been able to triumph over adversity?

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:28.760
That's what this program is going to try and examine.

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:33.800
Fewer than 90,000 gorillas are left in the world and they live in the tropical forests

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:40.080
of equatorial Africa, split into western and eastern species.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.040
The future of all gorillas is uncertain.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:54.280
They're threatened by loss of habitat and hunting, by disease and political instability.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:02:00.720
But surprisingly mountain gorillas, a subspecies of eastern gorilla, have been increasing despite

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.040
facing these same threats.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:07.880
That remarkable success is the subject of this story.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:14.480
Some 700 mountain gorillas live in the wild today and for 380 of them home is in the Varunga

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:23.520
volcanoes where the borders of Rwanda, Uganda and the Democratic Republic of Congo all meet.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:29.080
While those numbers may seem small, back in 1978 there were even fewer.

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:36.000
That's when I got the chance of a lifetime to film them for the BBC series Life on Earth.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:41.040
Spending time with these rare creatures was an unforgettable experience.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:52.560
There is more meaning and mutual understanding in exchanging of plants with a gorilla than

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:54.680
any other animal I know.

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:57.680
We see the world in the same way as they do.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:02:59.680
They walk alone on the ground as we do.

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:00.680
They're there.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:10.280
They're immensely more powerful than we are and so if there was a possibility of escaping

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:18.840
the human condition and living imaginatively in another creature's world, it must be with

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:24.240
the gorilla.

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:33.000
That intimate encounter not only moved me, it had a great effect on viewers everywhere.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:38.200
Mountain gorillas became iconic animals that people cared about.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:43.140
In order to understand how that happened, we need to examine their troubled relationship

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:47.720
with humanity.

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:53.600
The story begins over a century ago.

00:03:53.600 --> 00:04:03.000
In 1861, Paul Duchayle claimed to be the first white explorer to see the terrifying man-ape

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:11.040
of Africa in the wild and he described the encounter in this book, Explorations and Adventures

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.800
in Equatorial Africa.

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:18.460
This is how he describes the gorilla.

00:04:18.460 --> 00:04:25.120
His eyes began to flash fierce afire as we stood motionless on the defensive and the

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:30.800
crest of short hair which stands on his forehead began to twitch rapidly up and down while

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:37.800
his powerful fangs were shown as he again sent forth a thunderous roar.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:43.840
And now truly he reminded me of nothing but some hellish dream creature, a being of that

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:51.040
hideous order, half man, half beast, just as he began another of his roars, beating

00:04:51.040 --> 00:05:00.040
his breast in rage, we fired and killed him.

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:06.320
These terrifying tales inspired other explorers who wanted to see this half man, half beast

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:10.760
for themselves and to take specimens home as trophies.

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:17.880
Thousands of western lowland gorillas were killed for private collections.

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:23.880
Some were taken alive and those that survived transportation were then sentenced to a life

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:31.360
in captivity.

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:38.520
Mountain gorillas were not discovered by European explorers until 1902.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:43.480
A German, Captain Robert von Beringe, was travelling in the Wurundjer Mountains when

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:48.000
he came across two of them and shot them for scientific examination.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:59.120
The mountain gorilla was named after him, Gorilla Beringei.

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:05.600
In 1921, the taxidermist Carl Ackley from the American Museum of Natural History joined

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:07.720
the Gorilla Rush.

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:12.400
He travelled to the Wurundjer to film and collect mountain gorilla specimens and he

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:15.160
killed five of them.

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:18.600
But Carl Ackley's triumph was short lived.

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:24.360
In his autobiography, he describes the shame he felt as he looked at the animal he had

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:27.600
just killed.

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:33.420
As he lay at the base of the tree, it took all one's scientific ardour to keep from

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:36.240
feeling like a murderer.

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:42.560
He was a magnificent creature with the face of an amiable giant who would do no harm except

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:49.240
perhaps in self-defence or in defence of his friends.

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:57.480
Inspired by a new respect for gorillas, Carl Ackley persuaded the King of Belgium to declare

00:06:57.480 --> 00:07:01.240
the home of the mountain gorilla a national park.

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:11.880
So on April 25th 1925, the Wurundjer volcanoes became Africa's first national park.

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:18.160
The first step towards saving mountain gorillas had been taken.

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:23.920
But very little was known about the ecology, behaviour and population of mountain gorillas

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:27.320
until zoologists began detailed field studies.

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:34.920
A census in 1960 estimated that there were only 450 of them in the Wurundjer.

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:41.080
By 1967, when the American Diane Fosse came here to study the gorillas, that estimated

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:48.120
decreased dramatically to just 275.

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:54.440
On her arrival, Diane set up the Karisoki Research Centre and began what was to become the longest

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:58.520
and most detailed study of gorillas so far.

00:07:58.520 --> 00:08:02.240
Diane hardly seemed to be an ideal candidate for the job.

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:04.000
She was not a zoologist.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:09.200
She had wanted to become a vet but failed her exams and became an occupational therapist

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:14.220
instead.

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:20.000
She suffered from emphysema and a fear of heights, not ideal when working in thin air

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:23.000
on high mountains.

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:33.160
But her love of animals and her strong will made up for her lack of expertise.

00:08:33.160 --> 00:08:39.440
It soon became clear to Diane that the mountain gorillas were in trouble.

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:47.080
They were threatened not only by loss of habitat but by poachers.

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:53.960
The national park that had originally been created to protect them was failing to do

00:08:53.960 --> 00:08:55.480
so.

00:08:55.480 --> 00:09:00.800
Diane would not only have the task of studying these animals but she'd have to try and save

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:04.600
them from extinction.

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:08.920
She spent most of her time with one particular group and that was the one that we were to

00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:14.480
film for the BBC series Life on Earth.

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:19.560
Recently those of us who were on that trip got together to compare notes on just what

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:22.080
happened.

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:31.080
Not surprisingly perhaps after this long time our memories didn't always exactly coincide.

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:35.560
The director in charge of that particular programme was John Sparks.

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:38.000
It's not true.

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:44.320
I wrote the script and clearly if you're talking about the evolution of, leading to humanity,

00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:49.720
the opposable thumb, the thumb and forefinger giving the grip and I wrote in my script,

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:55.160
the original script, I wrote it with chimpanzees and you said, oh no not chimpanzees again,

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:57.240
why don't we do gorillas?

00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:02.600
And I said, because that's silly, gorillas.

00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:08.200
I've always been fascinated by primates anyway and I wanted the sea mountain gorillas so

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:12.040
this is how these things have evolved you see.

00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:15.760
I thought John's plan was rather over ambitious.

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:21.360
Mountain gorillas live 3000 metres high up in the Virunga volcanoes and are notoriously

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:23.880
difficult to approach.

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:29.140
Getting to them would mean carrying all our film equipment up 45 degree slopes through

00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:32.240
thick jungle.

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:36.920
And most problematical of all, there was no way that we'd be able to film them without

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:42.200
the help of Diane Fossey, the only person in the world who was studying them in the

00:10:42.200 --> 00:10:43.200
wild.

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:49.400
I'd heard of Diane Fossey and from what I'd heard I couldn't believe that Diane would

00:10:49.400 --> 00:10:54.600
allow a television crew to come in and I said you'll never get it and you wrote, and I know

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:57.640
you wrote a persuasive letter or something but you got the answer.

00:10:57.640 --> 00:11:03.120
I have to say it surprised us all that she wrote back a very nice letter saying you're

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:10.520
welcome and so we immediately made plans to launch an expedition there.

00:11:10.520 --> 00:11:16.280
A few weeks later we were on our way to the Virungas.

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:23.280
But things didn't turn out as expected.

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:28.120
Our guide we knew would be a researcher who'd been working as Diane's assistant for over

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:32.620
a year, a young Yorkshireman named Ian Redmond.

00:11:32.620 --> 00:11:40.760
When we arrived the first person we met down at Roan Garry was you Ian and you said I have

00:11:40.760 --> 00:11:43.840
got terrible news.

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:51.360
One Diane is very ill but two which is much more important that she is being destroyed

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:57.240
with sorrow because her favourite gorilla has just been murdered.

00:11:57.240 --> 00:12:05.360
The victim was Digit, a young male gorilla for whom Diane had a special affection.

00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:13.160
He had been killed eight days before we arrived on New Year's Eve 1977.

00:12:13.160 --> 00:12:19.200
He was 12 years old and had already gained the silver colouring of a mature male.

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:26.080
That meant that as a young silverback he was expected to act as bodyguard for the family.

00:12:26.080 --> 00:12:29.760
Days earlier poachers managed to get into the park.

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:35.520
They were setting snares for antelope but they were also after gorillas.

00:12:35.520 --> 00:12:42.600
Determined to protect his family Digit would have fought any intruder but his bravery was

00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:47.480
no defence against the poachers spears.

00:12:47.480 --> 00:12:51.680
Having killed him they cut off his head and his hands in order to sell them for a few

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:54.920
dollars as souvenirs.

00:12:54.920 --> 00:13:01.920
I had the worst experience in my life finding the body of someone I had known for over a

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:02.920
year.

00:13:02.920 --> 00:13:08.040
It was also clear that there had been a frenzy of violence because his body was covered with

00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:12.720
cuts and they had obviously just been in a blood lust.

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:15.900
They took his head and his hands and they left the rest of the body because people in

00:13:15.900 --> 00:13:17.160
Rwanda don't eat gorillas.

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:21.040
It is not a part of Africa where gorilla meat is favoured.

00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:24.160
So they had no use for the body and the only reason he was killed is because foreigners

00:13:24.160 --> 00:13:29.640
were buying bits of gorillas for souvenirs.

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:34.400
And then I had to go and find Diane.

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:43.520
We sat and talked right through to dawn actually and I felt Digit should become a martyr,

00:13:43.520 --> 00:13:47.920
that his death should be used to try and prevent other deaths.

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:51.680
And Diane was very worried that if we successfully raised a lot of money off the back of Digit's

00:13:51.680 --> 00:13:55.320
death that other gorillas would be killed to raise more money.

00:13:55.320 --> 00:14:02.480
But she did in the end agree to use Digit's death as a tool to raise awareness and raise

00:14:02.480 --> 00:14:09.080
funds to do what she called active conservation which is patrols out protecting the gorillas

00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:11.400
in the forest.

00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:16.960
It seemed the worst possible time to try and visit Diane and ask if we could make a natural

00:14:16.960 --> 00:14:20.400
history sequence for a television programme.

00:14:20.400 --> 00:14:28.000
If we had put a foot wrong, if we had said something that suggested that we would not

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:34.800
treat the gorillas with proper respect, she would have had no experience and said get

00:14:34.800 --> 00:14:35.800
out.

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:41.640
But when we arrived Diane decided that our filming trip could help publicise the plight

00:14:41.640 --> 00:14:46.680
of the gorillas and agreed that our filming could go ahead as planned.

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:50.800
She gave Martin Saunders special instructions on how to go about the job.

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:57.520
So I think we were very much on trial as it were, the first day we went out.

00:14:57.520 --> 00:15:03.640
I remember being told don't look them in the face, don't stare them in the eyes and don't

00:15:03.640 --> 00:15:06.840
stand up and crawl through the vegetation and grunt.

00:15:06.840 --> 00:15:12.960
I mean when Dickie and me saw them first I mean we started grunting boy, you know.

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:16.960
We were grunting and we had no intention of standing up that's for sure.

00:15:16.960 --> 00:15:20.880
The first view of a silverback, that's great.

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:27.040
Great swirl of vegetation and this huge animal sort of disappearing into the underground.

00:15:27.040 --> 00:15:34.600
And our first encounter with them you saw palm trees being snapped off and you know

00:15:34.600 --> 00:15:38.920
here were very very powerful animals and we were just about to approach them and you know

00:15:38.920 --> 00:15:41.920
there's just a little frisson of fear.

00:15:41.920 --> 00:15:45.840
Well maybe they are kind of real King Kongs.

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:50.880
We were all astonished to discover just how gentle these giants really were when they

00:15:50.880 --> 00:15:51.880
were undisturbed.

00:15:51.880 --> 00:15:57.840
I must say as a cameraman I was very surprised, I did not expect to get as close to the gorillas

00:15:57.840 --> 00:15:58.840
as we got.

00:15:58.840 --> 00:16:02.320
Well this was the gift that Diane gave the world.

00:16:02.320 --> 00:16:03.320
Yes absolutely.

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:07.360
The technique of winning the trust of completely wild gorillas.

00:16:07.360 --> 00:16:11.960
I mean it is quite difficult to keep your distance because it's not that you go to

00:16:11.960 --> 00:16:13.440
them is it, they come to you.

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:16.200
No no no and once you're accepted you become irrelevant.

00:16:16.200 --> 00:16:17.200
Yeah.

00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:21.000
I had a problem because one adult male saw a reflection in front of the camera, thought

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:24.920
it was another gorilla and came round in front, stuck his head and put his arm round me and

00:16:24.920 --> 00:16:26.520
it was impossible to film it because...

00:16:26.520 --> 00:16:36.800
That was one of the great moments of the filming actually Martin, to see this gorilla fingering

00:16:36.800 --> 00:16:40.720
the back of your head trying to see who it was.

00:16:40.720 --> 00:16:46.760
It was entirely thanks to Diane Fossey that we were able to get so close to the gorillas.

00:16:46.760 --> 00:16:51.920
After years of working to gain their confidence she had habituated them to her presence and

00:16:51.920 --> 00:16:55.920
they allowed her to sit alongside them without any concern.

00:16:55.920 --> 00:16:59.920
Puck has become very curious about my still camera.

00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:06.660
By the 1970s Diane's television reports and articles had made her famous worldwide.

00:17:06.660 --> 00:17:14.440
And through her people had come to know her gorillas as individuals with names and personalities.

00:17:14.440 --> 00:17:20.760
I'm always amused by three-year-old Pablo's pout which is unusual for gorillas.

00:17:20.760 --> 00:17:24.040
Diane knew little Pablo particularly well.

00:17:24.040 --> 00:17:29.880
He was always hanging around her intrigued by what she was up to.

00:17:29.880 --> 00:17:37.280
It was the same confident Pablo who chose to lie on me during the Life on Earth filming.

00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:45.680
David, what was your recollection of this moment?

00:17:45.680 --> 00:17:49.280
Oh, I suppose bliss really.

00:17:49.280 --> 00:17:51.600
You look quite happy David.

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:52.600
But you're grimacing.

00:17:52.600 --> 00:17:56.600
Well, I mean I'm grimacing because he's pulling my leg.

00:17:56.600 --> 00:18:00.280
One of them out of shot there is pulling my foot.

00:18:00.280 --> 00:18:03.880
These baby gorillas started taking off my shoes.

00:18:03.880 --> 00:18:08.400
When you can't talk about the opposable thumb and the importance in primate evolution of

00:18:08.400 --> 00:18:10.680
the grip, if somebody's taking off your shoes.

00:18:10.680 --> 00:18:13.160
Think of that somebody isn't it?

00:18:13.160 --> 00:18:16.080
It's two baby gorillas.

00:18:16.080 --> 00:18:17.080
So I thought well this is...

00:18:17.080 --> 00:18:19.080
Using their opposable thumb to undo your shoe laces.

00:18:19.080 --> 00:18:21.640
You can't actually do this.

00:18:21.640 --> 00:18:29.040
And so actually notions of primate evolution and the technicalities of digital grips vanished.

00:18:29.040 --> 00:18:37.680
And I just sort of lay there while this extraordinary experience.

00:18:37.680 --> 00:18:45.720
This was one of the amazing moments of that filming expedition where I had the expectation

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:49.880
of getting David with a few gorillas in the background and then to suddenly find he was

00:18:49.880 --> 00:18:55.480
surrounded by gorillas with youngsters sort of upending ornaments sticking their bottoms

00:18:55.480 --> 00:18:58.640
at his face and biting his knee.

00:18:58.640 --> 00:18:59.640
Unbelievable.

00:18:59.640 --> 00:19:07.080
That was certainly one of the most unforgettable moments of my career in making naturalistic

00:19:07.080 --> 00:19:08.080
films.

00:19:08.080 --> 00:19:16.400
It was a marvelous, blissful moment.

00:19:16.400 --> 00:19:21.880
Diane loved her gorillas as she might have loved her own children.

00:19:21.880 --> 00:19:26.760
The killing of Digit, her favorite, was a terrible blow for her.

00:19:26.760 --> 00:19:31.000
Furthermore, she had been severely ill for some time.

00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:36.040
She had a chest infection and was spitting blood.

00:19:36.040 --> 00:19:42.280
It was in this physical and emotional state that Diane asked for our help.

00:19:42.280 --> 00:19:49.880
She was in a frenzy of grief, wasn't she, really, but it was only after, on our last

00:19:49.880 --> 00:19:55.600
night there when we had filmed and got this remarkable footage, she invited me to her

00:19:55.600 --> 00:20:02.320
cabin and we talked for I don't know, an hour or so, in which she said, you must promise

00:20:02.320 --> 00:20:09.160
me that you will go back and you will organize fundraising.

00:20:09.160 --> 00:20:13.080
And I gave her that promise.

00:20:13.080 --> 00:20:18.800
So we set off on the return journey back to Kigali, the Rwandan capital, but there was

00:20:18.800 --> 00:20:21.320
further trouble ahead.

00:20:21.320 --> 00:20:25.400
We came down off the mountain to meet a truck in a field.

00:20:25.400 --> 00:20:26.880
We all got in this truck.

00:20:26.880 --> 00:20:32.040
I remember I sat in the front of the driver and we turned around this corner and there

00:20:32.040 --> 00:20:38.220
were some khaki-clad Africans in the road and the driver said, bandits, and put his

00:20:38.220 --> 00:20:41.640
foot down and we charged through these.

00:20:41.640 --> 00:20:46.880
And John and David and Dicky were on the back of the truck and I suddenly heard these bullets

00:20:46.880 --> 00:20:49.400
winging over their head.

00:20:49.400 --> 00:20:54.200
And it was then that it dawned on us that actually it wasn't bandits, it was actually

00:20:54.200 --> 00:20:58.960
the army that had been sent to arrest us because we seemed to have been caught in the middle

00:20:58.960 --> 00:21:04.680
of some sort of infight about making an anti-Rwandese publicity film about the way they weren't

00:21:04.680 --> 00:21:08.800
looking after the natural resources after Digit was killed.

00:21:08.800 --> 00:21:11.200
It was an alarming situation.

00:21:11.200 --> 00:21:16.080
Ian had stayed on the mountain with Diane and we didn't know who was who.

00:21:16.080 --> 00:21:19.920
But you don't argue with people carrying loaded rifles.

00:21:19.920 --> 00:21:27.400
I was hauled off the back and taken away to be strip searched and so on and you said to

00:21:27.400 --> 00:21:31.640
me, don't worry, I've changed the labels on the cans.

00:21:31.640 --> 00:21:32.640
That's right.

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:35.080
I heard the sound recorders and myself were on the back of the truck.

00:21:35.080 --> 00:21:38.960
And Dicky, I said, if they confiscate this film, we've been up that mountain in all

00:21:38.960 --> 00:21:40.400
that effort for nothing.

00:21:40.400 --> 00:21:44.560
We put exposed labels on unexposed rolls of film and we gave them the unexposed rolls

00:21:44.560 --> 00:21:45.560
of film.

00:21:45.560 --> 00:21:50.000
Dave and I were taken to a sort of barbie camp, was it?

00:21:50.000 --> 00:21:51.000
Bar bar enclosure.

00:21:51.000 --> 00:21:56.000
Bar bar enclosure and taken in to see someone who effectively started saying, well actually

00:21:56.000 --> 00:21:58.400
this is all a big mistake in the end, didn't it?

00:21:58.400 --> 00:22:02.960
And I think he wanted about two thousand dollars, didn't he, or a lot of money and I said I

00:22:02.960 --> 00:22:03.960
wasn't going to pay it.

00:22:03.960 --> 00:22:04.960
And David said, pay it.

00:22:04.960 --> 00:22:05.960
I said, I'm not going to.

00:22:05.960 --> 00:22:06.960
And I argued with it.

00:22:06.960 --> 00:22:09.960
You threatened to kill him then?

00:22:09.960 --> 00:22:11.320
Yeah, that's right.

00:22:11.320 --> 00:22:12.320
Yeah.

00:22:12.320 --> 00:22:15.280
So anyway, we jumped into a taxi and went straight to the airport.

00:22:15.280 --> 00:22:21.000
We got on to our plane and when I saw the runway disappear behind us, I thought, thank

00:22:21.000 --> 00:22:22.000
God.

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:23.000
That's right.

00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:24.000
Yeah.

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:25.000
Yeah.

00:22:25.000 --> 00:22:26.000
Yeah.

00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:35.200
We now had to continue with the rest of the filming for Life on Earth elsewhere in Africa.

00:22:35.200 --> 00:22:41.520
Back in Rwanda, Diane was spending less time on scientific research and more on her war

00:22:41.520 --> 00:22:45.120
against the poachers.

00:22:45.120 --> 00:22:51.280
She established the first effective anti-poaching patrols in the Verunkas, patrols which eventually

00:22:51.280 --> 00:23:01.100
joined forces with government rangers and continue to this day.

00:23:01.100 --> 00:23:05.800
When they caught poachers, they brought them back to Diane for questioning and only then

00:23:05.800 --> 00:23:08.360
handed them over to the authorities.

00:23:08.360 --> 00:23:13.280
But Diane was also known to have more controversial methods for deterring poachers.

00:23:13.280 --> 00:23:19.040
There were stories of her attempting to terrify them using Halloween masks and even kidnapping

00:23:19.040 --> 00:23:20.960
their children.

00:23:20.960 --> 00:23:29.440
But she realized that if she didn't take extreme action fast, there would be no gorillas left.

00:23:29.440 --> 00:23:32.400
It wasn't just poachers that Diane had to worry about.

00:23:32.400 --> 00:23:38.320
The gorillas' forest home on which they depended was rapidly disappearing.

00:23:38.320 --> 00:23:44.720
It had already been reduced to a tiny island of forest in the middle of an immense sea

00:23:44.720 --> 00:23:48.120
of humanity.

00:23:48.120 --> 00:23:55.680
Now the increasing human population was threatening the little patch of forest that was left.

00:23:55.680 --> 00:24:01.600
It was being cleared to make way for fields in which to grow pyrethrum, a kind of chrysanthemum

00:24:01.600 --> 00:24:07.080
that had suddenly become very valuable as an eco-friendly alternative to the insecticide

00:24:07.080 --> 00:24:11.240
DDT, which was poisoning so much wildlife in Europe.

00:24:11.240 --> 00:24:17.380
So paradoxically, protecting European eagles was now threatening African apes.

00:24:17.380 --> 00:24:23.520
When the European community sponsored the Rwandan government to remove 40% of the park

00:24:23.520 --> 00:24:30.680
to grow pyrethrum so that we had this biodegradable insecticide for our crops, they were actually

00:24:30.680 --> 00:24:33.560
going to take that limit up to the 10,000 foot mark.

00:24:33.560 --> 00:24:37.320
Diane told me she saw the plans and it was going to be coming up to Karisoki, right up

00:24:37.320 --> 00:24:38.320
to the research center.

00:24:38.320 --> 00:24:39.320
It would have been fields.

00:24:39.320 --> 00:24:40.320
Good Lord.

00:24:40.320 --> 00:24:44.320
And she fought that in her in an invisible way, undoubtedly pounding tables and dominating

00:24:44.320 --> 00:24:49.400
people and got the limit of the park redrawn at the 8,500 foot level.

00:24:49.400 --> 00:24:54.480
And if she hadn't done that, for sure, there would not be.

00:24:54.480 --> 00:25:01.800
Diane was single-handedly battling to save the gorillas despite her ill health.

00:25:01.800 --> 00:25:08.480
If there was ever a time when she needed outside help, it was now.

00:25:08.480 --> 00:25:12.880
Back in Britain, the Life on Earth series was broadcast on television.

00:25:12.880 --> 00:25:18.040
It was a great success and eventually seen by 500 million people worldwide.

00:25:18.040 --> 00:25:22.040
The gorilla episode was arguably the most popular sequence of all.

00:25:22.040 --> 00:25:26.360
And many people began to feel they wanted to help in the struggle to save these beautiful

00:25:26.360 --> 00:25:27.360
creatures.

00:25:27.360 --> 00:25:35.280
I hadn't forgotten my promise to Diane to help raise funds for mountain gorilla conservation.

00:25:35.280 --> 00:25:42.360
When I came back, we got new and Sandy Harcourt and Kelly Stewart and the Fauna and Fauna

00:25:42.360 --> 00:25:49.320
International and we set up the gorilla mountain gorilla project there and then, which started

00:25:49.320 --> 00:25:52.040
to raise money and eventually it raised a lot of money.

00:25:52.040 --> 00:25:57.360
And continues in a new guise to this day.

00:25:57.360 --> 00:26:02.840
Bill Webber was one of the key players responsible for implementing the mountain gorilla project

00:26:02.840 --> 00:26:03.840
in Rwanda.

00:26:03.840 --> 00:26:09.520
When he began, more than half the people told him they thought the area should be cleared

00:26:09.520 --> 00:26:11.800
for agriculture.

00:26:11.800 --> 00:26:21.160
Convincing the local people of the value of gorilla conservation wasn't going to be easy.

00:26:21.160 --> 00:26:26.920
Mountain Gorilla Project started officially in the summer of 1979 and had three main components.

00:26:26.920 --> 00:26:31.960
One was to improve park protection and security, hire more guards, train them better, start

00:26:31.960 --> 00:26:36.560
an education program so that you not only had millions of people around the world who

00:26:36.560 --> 00:26:40.920
cared about gorillas, but that you had at least thousands of Rwandans who knew and cared

00:26:40.920 --> 00:26:41.920
about gorillas.

00:26:41.920 --> 00:26:48.960
But what became the linchpin of the mountain gorilla project was the tourism program.

00:26:48.960 --> 00:26:53.200
We went to the park service and said you can make a lot more money off of tourism if you

00:26:53.200 --> 00:26:57.440
set up a program that has gorilla-based tourism at its heart.

00:26:57.440 --> 00:27:03.920
The term ecotourism didn't exist at the time, but it really was a prototype of that.

00:27:03.920 --> 00:27:08.860
There's just nothing quite like being in and among mountain gorillas.

00:27:08.860 --> 00:27:11.840
People will pay practically whatever they ask here in Rwanda.

00:27:11.840 --> 00:27:15.640
Mountain gorillas are exceedingly tolerant of a human presence.

00:27:15.640 --> 00:27:21.520
They just seem to almost love having the company.

00:27:21.520 --> 00:27:27.200
But the mountain gorilla project faced opposition from two sources.

00:27:27.200 --> 00:27:31.880
First of all, the Ministry of Agriculture, with European funding, was about to take another

00:27:31.880 --> 00:27:36.240
one-third of the park, 5,000 hectares, for a cattle raising project.

00:27:36.240 --> 00:27:39.560
And they were a very strong ministry, and the park service was not very strong in those

00:27:39.560 --> 00:27:40.560
days.

00:27:40.560 --> 00:27:46.360
Surprisingly, the second source of opposition to the mountain gorilla project was Diane

00:27:46.360 --> 00:27:48.240
Fosse herself.

00:27:48.240 --> 00:27:53.280
I thought she would welcome the support and the funds, but she didn't feel that the education

00:27:53.280 --> 00:28:00.720
projects were a funding priority and saw gorilla tourism as more of a hindrance than a help.

00:28:00.720 --> 00:28:05.040
Diane believed that the gorillas ought to be protected for their own values.

00:28:05.040 --> 00:28:08.560
I think that's a very noble sentiment, but it wasn't working.

00:28:08.560 --> 00:28:12.640
While she was here, the gorilla population had been nearly halved, 40% of the park had

00:28:12.640 --> 00:28:14.080
been cleared.

00:28:14.080 --> 00:28:17.520
We felt you had to come up with an alternative, and that's what the mountain gorilla project

00:28:17.520 --> 00:28:19.160
offered.

00:28:19.160 --> 00:28:24.360
I think although it was often portrayed as an opposite point of view, Diane, active conservation

00:28:24.360 --> 00:28:28.880
versus the mountain gorilla project and its long-term view, both were right.

00:28:28.880 --> 00:28:31.800
You can't have one without the other.

00:28:31.800 --> 00:28:36.040
But in terms of priorities, if you've got a thousand pounds, what do you spend it on?

00:28:36.040 --> 00:28:40.380
Education that might help the next generation while gorillas are dying today, or patrols

00:28:40.380 --> 00:28:46.840
that protect the gorillas now, and then try and find some more money for the future education.

00:28:46.840 --> 00:28:50.640
I think Diane's contribution as a conservationist is fairly limited.

00:28:50.640 --> 00:28:52.140
She didn't come to be a conservationist.

00:28:52.140 --> 00:28:53.440
She came to study the gorillas.

00:28:53.440 --> 00:28:55.320
She was forced into trying to protect them.

00:28:55.320 --> 00:28:57.920
She did some things that she thought would help.

00:28:57.920 --> 00:28:59.400
I don't think they were terribly effective.

00:28:59.400 --> 00:29:01.520
I think some of them were counterproductive.

00:29:01.520 --> 00:29:06.680
I think it required people with a different vision and a different approach to make conservation

00:29:06.680 --> 00:29:09.160
work in this park and for the gorillas.

00:29:09.160 --> 00:29:12.480
I've always believed we're supporting exactly the same mission as Diane.

00:29:12.480 --> 00:29:16.200
We just use different techniques.

00:29:16.200 --> 00:29:21.700
Although Diane Fosse has many critics, she certainly succeeded in stimulating worldwide

00:29:21.700 --> 00:29:28.440
interest in mountain gorillas and their plight.

00:29:28.440 --> 00:29:31.680
All too easily the mountain gorilla may become extinct.

00:29:31.680 --> 00:29:41.080
That's something we cannot afford to forget.

00:29:41.080 --> 00:29:46.600
Diane's fame had led to a bestselling book, Gorillas in the Mist, which was published

00:29:46.600 --> 00:29:49.160
in 1983.

00:29:49.160 --> 00:29:54.440
Her story sparked an interest in Hollywood and production started on a feature film about

00:29:54.440 --> 00:30:00.960
her life.

00:30:00.960 --> 00:30:06.260
It was a tragedy that just as the world was becoming concerned about mountain gorillas

00:30:06.260 --> 00:30:11.520
and international efforts were uniting to halt their decline, Diane was no longer there

00:30:11.520 --> 00:30:14.360
to witness the progress.

00:30:14.360 --> 00:30:18.520
She died on the night of December the 26th, 1985.

00:30:18.520 --> 00:30:21.440
Her death was not natural.

00:30:21.440 --> 00:30:28.960
An American naturalist working in the central African state of Rwanda has been found murdered.

00:30:28.960 --> 00:30:33.320
Police said the body of Miss Diane Fosse was discovered at the Karisoki Research Institute,

00:30:33.320 --> 00:30:34.800
which she founded herself.

00:30:34.800 --> 00:30:41.080
No details were given about how she met her death or who her killers were.

00:30:41.080 --> 00:30:45.160
Ian Redmond was back in England when he heard the terrible news.

00:30:45.160 --> 00:30:47.760
I traveled out to Rwanda.

00:30:47.760 --> 00:30:51.320
We went up to her cabin.

00:30:51.320 --> 00:30:52.320
She had just been buried.

00:30:52.320 --> 00:30:56.600
I had missed the funeral, but her blood stains were still on the carpet.

00:30:56.600 --> 00:31:02.200
There was a chunk where a machete had hit a bedside table and Diane's hair was caught

00:31:02.200 --> 00:31:03.200
in a splinter.

00:31:03.200 --> 00:31:05.720
It was the scene of the crime.

00:31:05.720 --> 00:31:11.800
But we don't know who did it and the murders never been solved.

00:31:11.800 --> 00:31:15.560
She was standing in the way of certain individuals making money.

00:31:15.560 --> 00:31:18.880
Whether it was because they were making money through the illegal bushmeat trade or the

00:31:18.880 --> 00:31:24.640
gold smuggling trade or someone's aspirations to turn Karisoki into a tourist camp and make

00:31:24.640 --> 00:31:26.040
a lot of money that way.

00:31:26.040 --> 00:31:29.040
If you stand in the way of someone who is ruthless, who wants to make a lot of money,

00:31:29.040 --> 00:31:36.680
then it's not that surprising that she was killed.

00:31:36.680 --> 00:31:42.440
Diane Fosse's grave now lies alongside those of her guerrilla friends.

00:31:42.440 --> 00:31:48.460
She spent 19 years researching and campaigning passionately on behalf of the guerrillas and

00:31:48.460 --> 00:31:50.920
ultimately gave her life for them.

00:31:50.920 --> 00:31:56.440
As her gravestone says, no one loves guerrillas more.

00:31:56.440 --> 00:32:01.040
All of us absolutely recognise that if it wasn't for Diane, the mountain guerrillas

00:32:01.040 --> 00:32:03.800
probably wouldn't be there at all now.

00:32:03.800 --> 00:32:09.040
I think her short-term measures, which some people see as politically incorrect or inappropriate

00:32:09.040 --> 00:32:15.340
or not the best way forward, were what held the ground until the more thoughtful and better

00:32:15.340 --> 00:32:18.360
planned long-term measures were put in place.

00:32:18.360 --> 00:32:22.520
She was a fantastic role model for millions of people and inspired millions of people.

00:32:22.520 --> 00:32:27.200
I think she deserves a better reputation in science than she has.

00:32:27.200 --> 00:32:33.880
Diane started what is less glamorous but is essential to have long-term monitoring of

00:32:33.880 --> 00:32:35.440
populations.

00:32:35.440 --> 00:32:43.120
We now have life histories of individuals, families, changes in groups, births, deaths,

00:32:43.120 --> 00:32:46.960
infidelities, all of this for guerrillas ever since the late 1960s.

00:32:46.960 --> 00:32:52.800
That's a great contribution.

00:32:52.800 --> 00:32:57.600
Diane had been murdered, but the feature film about her life still went ahead.

00:32:57.600 --> 00:33:04.040
Sigourney Weaver played her character in Gorillas in the Mist.

00:33:04.040 --> 00:33:09.400
To mark the 20th anniversary of the death of Diane Fosse, Sigourney Weaver returned

00:33:09.400 --> 00:33:15.200
to Rwanda to pay her respects and find out how Diane's legacy continues to help the

00:33:15.200 --> 00:33:19.120
mountain guerrillas.

00:33:19.120 --> 00:33:28.080
Playing Diane, I think, gave me such an experience of how much of a difference one individual

00:33:28.080 --> 00:33:29.080
can make.

00:33:29.080 --> 00:33:35.840
I'd never played anyone real before and I was so moved by the fact that here is this

00:33:35.840 --> 00:33:41.800
woman who came all by herself, having never really been to Africa, and started researching

00:33:41.800 --> 00:33:47.200
the mountain guerrillas and really started this whole movement, not just to study them

00:33:47.200 --> 00:33:51.560
but also to rescue them from what was certain extinction.

00:33:51.560 --> 00:33:56.760
And I think that it's a very inspiring story and I think I appreciate it even more now

00:33:56.760 --> 00:34:03.840
than I did when I was playing her, how courageous she had to be and how determined and how much

00:34:03.840 --> 00:34:09.200
she must have loved these animals.

00:34:09.200 --> 00:34:15.840
The movie Gorillas in the Mist was not only a huge box office success, it also persuaded

00:34:15.840 --> 00:34:21.440
millions worldwide to care about mountain guerrillas.

00:34:21.440 --> 00:34:26.520
Sigourney Weaver herself became determined to do whatever she could to help.

00:34:26.520 --> 00:34:32.480
Since 1987, she's been campaigning to save them as the honorary chair of the Diane Fosse

00:34:32.480 --> 00:34:45.200
Guerrilla Fund International.

00:34:45.200 --> 00:34:50.360
Public support for guerrilla conservation and tourism increased following the movie,

00:34:50.360 --> 00:35:01.220
and mountain guerrillas became the latest must-see animal.

00:35:01.220 --> 00:35:08.200
In the 80s and early 90s, their population steadily grew to about 320 individuals and

00:35:08.200 --> 00:35:12.760
their future was looking promising.

00:35:12.760 --> 00:35:23.200
But this period of stability and growth was about to come to a sudden end.

00:35:23.200 --> 00:35:28.680
The countries which between them share the mountain guerrillas, Rwanda, Uganda and the

00:35:28.680 --> 00:35:37.360
Democratic Republic of Congo, formerly Zaire, have a history of political turmoil.

00:35:37.360 --> 00:35:44.680
When civil war broke out in Rwanda, caused by rivalries between extremist Hutus and Tutsis,

00:35:44.680 --> 00:35:52.480
guerrilla tourism came to an abrupt halt and the future of the animals became very uncertain.

00:35:52.480 --> 00:35:58.880
At the start of the war, both sides, realizing the guerrillas' economic value, issued assurances

00:35:58.880 --> 00:36:02.280
that they would not begin.

00:36:02.280 --> 00:36:07.240
But when the war escalated to horrific levels, no one was safe.

00:36:07.240 --> 00:36:15.040
In 1994, a million people were killed in a hundred days of genocidal slaughter.

00:36:15.040 --> 00:36:20.640
The staff involved in guerrilla research and conservation were forced to flee and several

00:36:20.640 --> 00:36:23.280
died.

00:36:23.280 --> 00:36:30.520
The region became the scene of the worst humanitarian crisis since the Second World War.

00:36:30.520 --> 00:36:35.880
There were two million refugees at camps bordering the park and the forest was plundered for

00:36:35.880 --> 00:36:47.720
firewood and bush meat.

00:36:47.720 --> 00:36:54.520
In August 1994, only days after the war had come to an end, Ian Redmond and Dieter Stechlis

00:36:54.520 --> 00:36:59.920
from the Diane Fosse Guerrilla Fund International went back to Rwanda.

00:36:59.920 --> 00:37:04.760
Their plan was to rescue the park staff who had been forced to flee and were now in refugee

00:37:04.760 --> 00:37:07.080
camps in Zaire.

00:37:07.080 --> 00:37:11.760
They were also anxious to find out how the guerrillas had been affected by the war.

00:37:11.760 --> 00:37:16.880
Their hazardous journey was filmed by the BBC for a nature special.

00:37:16.880 --> 00:37:26.920
We drove down from Uganda, across the border, not knowing what we were going to find.

00:37:26.920 --> 00:37:31.920
Travelling through Rwanda, through the roadblocks, past the bodies in the ditches, so it was

00:37:31.920 --> 00:37:35.600
a country that was at stank of death.

00:37:35.600 --> 00:37:40.200
Every one of my Rwandan friends had lost some member of their family, some of the most of

00:37:40.200 --> 00:37:42.240
their family.

00:37:42.240 --> 00:37:52.400
And that really was an awful period.

00:37:52.400 --> 00:37:57.760
It was obviously the surrounding area was being stripped of its forest, of its wood.

00:37:57.760 --> 00:38:01.200
And you can't blame the people that it's cold, it's wet, they need to cook food and keep

00:38:01.200 --> 00:38:02.200
warm.

00:38:02.200 --> 00:38:07.000
But in just trying to survive, they were destroying the surrounding forest.

00:38:07.000 --> 00:38:12.800
And we knew that hungry people would be buying bushmeat, and if we didn't get some sort of

00:38:12.800 --> 00:38:17.680
protection in there quickly, then the park would be awash with snares and hunters killing

00:38:17.680 --> 00:38:21.480
antelope and buffalo, even if they weren't targeting guerrillas.

00:38:21.480 --> 00:38:27.600
So we wanted to get the conservation work restarted before the poachers got organised,

00:38:27.600 --> 00:38:32.540
and they would be quick on the mark.

00:38:32.540 --> 00:38:38.400
After days of searching, Ian Redmond and Dieter Stecklis finally tracked down some of the

00:38:38.400 --> 00:38:39.760
park staff.

00:38:39.760 --> 00:38:43.880
It was critical that they were brought back to the park quickly to prevent the widespread

00:38:43.880 --> 00:38:48.920
killing of guerrillas.

00:38:48.920 --> 00:38:54.040
The knowledge that's in the heads of all the trackers and guides and rangers is what's

00:38:54.040 --> 00:38:56.080
the future of those guerrillas depend on.

00:38:56.080 --> 00:39:06.680
And so looking after our friends and getting them back to work was one thing.

00:39:06.680 --> 00:39:11.640
And to go into the forest, which many people would see as being kind of an alien environment,

00:39:11.640 --> 00:39:15.560
hostile, felt actually like walking back into the Garden of Eden.

00:39:15.560 --> 00:39:16.560
It was just wonderful.

00:39:16.560 --> 00:39:18.520
This sort of oppression lifted.

00:39:18.520 --> 00:39:24.800
You're back with the smells and the sounds of the forest.

00:39:24.800 --> 00:39:29.080
And we got up to the research centre and found that the buildings had been trashed and that

00:39:29.080 --> 00:39:32.240
possessions had been stolen or destroyed and it was a mess.

00:39:32.240 --> 00:39:35.120
But there were still cabins there then.

00:39:35.120 --> 00:39:37.940
It was heartbreaking for Ian and Dieter.

00:39:37.940 --> 00:39:40.360
The research centre was in ruins.

00:39:40.360 --> 00:39:45.720
But their biggest concern was whether any of the park's guerrillas had been killed.

00:39:45.720 --> 00:39:50.600
We had somewhere to stay and we went out the next day to find one of the guerrilla groups.

00:39:50.600 --> 00:40:00.200
Ian was filled with apprehension as he hiked in search of the guerrillas.

00:40:00.200 --> 00:40:03.000
And there they were, just going about their business in the forest.

00:40:03.000 --> 00:40:05.640
It was wonderful to see them.

00:40:05.640 --> 00:40:10.960
To his great relief, he had been able to locate and identify more and more of his guerrilla

00:40:10.960 --> 00:40:16.400
friends one by one.

00:40:16.400 --> 00:40:20.520
Over the years of unrest, more than 20 had disappeared.

00:40:20.520 --> 00:40:26.200
But that was far fewer than most people had expected.

00:40:26.200 --> 00:40:30.840
It may well be that most of the guerrillas had been able to flee and so had avoided the

00:40:30.840 --> 00:40:31.840
crossfire.

00:40:31.840 --> 00:40:38.520
But now they were back and Ian and Dieter were able to reinstate park staff and restart

00:40:38.520 --> 00:40:43.560
patrols by guards before poachers were able to resume their hunting.

00:40:43.560 --> 00:40:49.800
The guerrillas were now protected once again.

00:40:49.800 --> 00:40:53.720
The feeling at this stage was just one of enormous relief to find that Pablo's group

00:40:53.720 --> 00:40:56.360
as well as Titus's were basically intact.

00:40:56.360 --> 00:41:03.800
That they survived the worst of the war and seemed to be okay.

00:41:03.800 --> 00:41:08.720
It was very much the feeling that the guerrillas really had a lesson for us.

00:41:08.720 --> 00:41:12.880
That we humans should perhaps take a lesson out of their book and stop killing our neighbors

00:41:12.880 --> 00:41:18.720
and our friends and concentrate on the important things in life like eating and playing and

00:41:18.720 --> 00:41:19.720
making babies.

00:41:19.720 --> 00:41:26.840
It seemed like a good omen that we'd got there and were able to protect them again.

00:41:26.840 --> 00:41:36.120
And it's extraordinary how in only 10 years Rwanda has repaired itself, healed itself.

00:41:36.120 --> 00:41:39.440
And yes of course there are still conflicts, of course there are still people who are trying

00:41:39.440 --> 00:41:42.240
to get over the terrible things that happened.

00:41:42.240 --> 00:41:46.520
But it is very much the feeling of a country that is pulling itself together and moving

00:41:46.520 --> 00:41:47.520
forward.

00:41:47.520 --> 00:41:51.320
And what is wonderful in terms of the guerrilla story is that the guerrillas are right at

00:41:51.320 --> 00:41:55.320
the center of that progressive look forward.

00:41:55.320 --> 00:42:01.800
Ian's nature special was screened on New Year's Day 1995.

00:42:01.800 --> 00:42:11.640
Viewers were reassured that the mountain guerrillas had survived the war with minimum casualties.

00:42:11.640 --> 00:42:30.240
In the following years tourism began to increase again as security in the region improved.

00:42:30.240 --> 00:42:35.720
By the year 2000 there were 360 mountain guerrillas.

00:42:35.720 --> 00:42:42.320
The next BBC primate series, Cousins, broadcast the same year showed viewers that the mountain

00:42:42.320 --> 00:42:46.080
guerrillas were indeed thriving.

00:42:46.080 --> 00:42:55.520
Primatologist Dr Charlotte Erlenbrook went to Rwanda to film with the guerrillas.

00:42:55.520 --> 00:43:03.440
The highlight of the whole trip of course was meeting this newborn baby gorilla.

00:43:03.440 --> 00:43:05.040
We were so lucky.

00:43:05.040 --> 00:43:11.600
It was literally the night before we arrived we heard the news that a baby gorilla had

00:43:11.600 --> 00:43:12.600
been born.

00:43:12.600 --> 00:43:19.320
And it was such a fantastic sign of hope I felt because Rwanda had been devastated by

00:43:19.320 --> 00:43:23.440
war and against all odds the guerrillas had survived.

00:43:23.440 --> 00:43:29.560
They continued their lives in the forest and here was this new arrival, a new generation.

00:43:29.560 --> 00:43:34.880
Such an optimistic sign for the future of those mountain guerrillas I felt.

00:43:34.880 --> 00:43:39.720
The fact that this baby had been born to a mother who had not only survived the war

00:43:39.720 --> 00:43:51.320
but had lost a hand and a foot in poachers snares was a cause for great celebration.

00:43:51.320 --> 00:43:55.040
It was very touching because the park rangers got together and decided that they wanted

00:43:55.040 --> 00:43:58.360
to name the guerrilla BBC after our film crew.

00:43:58.360 --> 00:44:00.520
It was a real honour.

00:44:00.520 --> 00:44:05.040
So when we got down from the mountain that day we all cracked open some beers and wet

00:44:05.040 --> 00:44:08.560
the baby's head metaphorically of course.

00:44:08.560 --> 00:44:14.880
And no it was lovely and of course it made us feel that we had a very very special connection

00:44:14.880 --> 00:44:18.240
with this baby.

00:44:18.240 --> 00:44:24.200
The birth of baby BBC was a source of optimism but the threats to the guerrillas had not

00:44:24.200 --> 00:44:29.160
gone away.

00:44:29.160 --> 00:44:35.120
On the 9th of May 2002 gum shots were heard in the park.

00:44:35.120 --> 00:44:39.960
Two adult female guerrillas, Empanga and Muraha, were found dead.

00:44:39.960 --> 00:44:46.480
Muraha's infant was still clinging to her mother's dead body.

00:44:46.480 --> 00:44:54.560
But Empanga's infant, baby BBC was missing.

00:44:54.560 --> 00:45:00.840
It was absolutely devastating when we got the news that BBC had actually been kidnapped

00:45:00.840 --> 00:45:06.240
and Empanga, her mother, had been killed.

00:45:06.240 --> 00:45:09.840
To this day we don't know what happened to baby BBC.

00:45:09.840 --> 00:45:15.120
Very very few mountain guerrillas make it, especially when they're ripped from their

00:45:15.120 --> 00:45:17.520
mother's arms at the age of two.

00:45:17.520 --> 00:45:21.840
Just the trauma of that experience alone is enough usually to kill them.

00:45:21.840 --> 00:45:29.200
I think it's highly unlikely that she would have made it which is desperately sad.

00:45:29.200 --> 00:45:33.880
The capture of baby BBC was not a one-off case.

00:45:33.880 --> 00:45:38.760
Baby guerrillas are still being snatched from the wild despite the fact that the commercial

00:45:38.760 --> 00:45:48.040
trade in all apes is prohibited by the Convention on the International Trade of Endangered Species.

00:45:48.040 --> 00:45:53.440
One baby mountain gorilla that was recently stolen was rescued and is being kept near

00:45:53.440 --> 00:45:56.920
the Rwandan Park headquarters.

00:45:56.920 --> 00:45:59.920
This is three-year-old Misha.

00:45:59.920 --> 00:46:04.740
Park staff were tipped off that she was being offered for sale and rescued her from where

00:46:04.740 --> 00:46:07.680
she was being secretly kept.

00:46:07.680 --> 00:46:12.320
Chris Whittier, a vet from the Mountain Guerrilla Veterinary Project, is responsible for her

00:46:12.320 --> 00:46:14.320
care.

00:46:14.320 --> 00:46:18.120
The police and the National Parks authorities did an undercover operation and managed to

00:46:18.120 --> 00:46:22.840
find where she was and arrested a number of poachers.

00:46:22.840 --> 00:46:26.800
Up until that time she had been in a cave inside of a sack so it was pretty horrible

00:46:26.800 --> 00:46:27.800
conditions.

00:46:27.800 --> 00:46:33.040
By the time Misha was rescued she was very traumatized.

00:46:33.040 --> 00:46:37.280
It's likely that she would have witnessed the murder of some of her family as poachers

00:46:37.280 --> 00:46:42.880
would almost certainly have had to kill them in order to steal their baby.

00:46:42.880 --> 00:46:46.240
Caring for mountain gorillas in captivity is not easy.

00:46:46.240 --> 00:46:47.240
Few survive.

00:46:47.240 --> 00:46:52.440
But the vets here are specialists in gorilla medicine and are doing everything they can

00:46:52.440 --> 00:46:55.880
to ensure her survival.

00:46:55.880 --> 00:47:02.160
Misha is currently the only captive mountain gorilla in the world and often she needs 24-hour

00:47:02.160 --> 00:47:04.400
care and attention.

00:47:04.400 --> 00:47:09.700
She's evaluated periodically to look at her social adjustment and how confident she is

00:47:09.700 --> 00:47:11.600
and those sort of things.

00:47:11.600 --> 00:47:16.680
And right now the assessment is that she's doing well, she's come a long way, but that

00:47:16.680 --> 00:47:22.240
she needs a little bit more time still to really get up to a normal behavioral level

00:47:22.240 --> 00:47:24.360
of a gorilla her age.

00:47:24.360 --> 00:47:28.000
The team caring for Misha have a moral dilemma.

00:47:28.000 --> 00:47:33.280
It seems wrong to keep her in captivity longer than absolutely necessary, but releasing her

00:47:33.280 --> 00:47:35.400
into the wild is very risky.

00:47:35.400 --> 00:47:42.360
She could be rejected by other gorilla groups, injured or even killed.

00:47:42.360 --> 00:47:46.500
Our hope is that she will get back into the wild and become a healthy individual and contribute

00:47:46.500 --> 00:47:47.500
to the population.

00:47:47.500 --> 00:47:51.300
Unfortunately, that's easier said than done.

00:47:51.300 --> 00:47:53.420
Every gorilla is precious.

00:47:53.420 --> 00:48:02.640
Their populations are still so low that the loss of a single one could be critical.

00:48:02.640 --> 00:48:06.680
But the mountain gorilla's fame will help its survival.

00:48:06.680 --> 00:48:16.320
A gorilla kidnapped today is a concern nationally and internationally.

00:48:16.320 --> 00:48:21.800
Thanks to the economic value of gorilla tourism, these animals are now recognized as one of

00:48:21.800 --> 00:48:25.800
the region's most valuable assets.

00:48:25.800 --> 00:48:34.840
In Rwanda, over 8,000 visitors every year pay up to $375 each to see the gorillas.

00:48:34.840 --> 00:48:39.520
The annual revenue generated is over $2 million.

00:48:39.520 --> 00:48:44.120
And the local community stands to benefit directly from the gorillas as the government

00:48:44.120 --> 00:48:48.700
has pledged to give them 5% of the park fees.

00:48:48.700 --> 00:48:53.760
Mountain gorillas are very, very important to the Rwandan economy and to the Rwandan people.

00:48:53.760 --> 00:48:56.640
Now for us, it is an identity.

00:48:56.640 --> 00:48:58.760
You say Rwanda, then you say gorillas.

00:48:58.760 --> 00:49:02.280
They are the flagship of tourism in Rwanda.

00:49:02.280 --> 00:49:09.480
Now tourism is placed as number three, as one of the major foreign exchange animals.

00:49:09.480 --> 00:49:16.660
Also, when you come specifically here to the Virunga, it's helping us in conservation.

00:49:16.660 --> 00:49:23.720
We could not have saved this habitat if there were no gorillas there.

00:49:23.720 --> 00:49:28.760
It's no surprise to me that gorilla tourism is so popular.

00:49:28.760 --> 00:49:32.920
Spending time with gorillas is an experience like no other.

00:49:32.920 --> 00:49:36.360
And they are as interested in us as we are in them.

00:49:36.360 --> 00:49:40.480
Ian has his own theories on why this is so.

00:49:40.480 --> 00:49:43.200
I think they're intrigued by how little arcanine teeth are.

00:49:43.200 --> 00:49:44.200
Is that right?

00:49:44.200 --> 00:49:45.200
I think that they're...

00:49:45.200 --> 00:49:46.200
Genuinely so.

00:49:46.200 --> 00:49:47.200
Yes, I think that...

00:49:47.200 --> 00:49:50.720
Apart from the fact that we haven't actually got a beard and big black hair.

00:49:50.720 --> 00:49:55.480
No, but you look and smell like an adult male.

00:49:55.480 --> 00:49:58.440
We smell pretty much the same as them, especially when we've been sweating through the undergrowth

00:49:58.440 --> 00:49:59.440
for a few hours.

00:49:59.440 --> 00:50:05.400
When we were with them, it all suddenly came back to me, sort of visions of school, because

00:50:05.400 --> 00:50:12.120
I thought, this smells like a rugby, sort of changing room after a hot, sweaty match.

00:50:12.120 --> 00:50:13.120
Without the liniment.

00:50:13.120 --> 00:50:14.120
Yes.

00:50:14.120 --> 00:50:19.320
You are constantly being reminded that you're there, you're close relations, because they

00:50:19.320 --> 00:50:24.400
behave in ways and react in ways which are very reminiscent of human.

00:50:24.400 --> 00:50:29.680
I mean, the problem is that it's almost too much, because you are, if you're not careful,

00:50:29.680 --> 00:50:35.480
you identify too much and you interpret too much in your own terms, and you probably get

00:50:35.480 --> 00:50:38.000
it wrong.

00:50:38.000 --> 00:50:46.760
But visitors are no longer allowed to get as close as I was to Pablo back in 1978.

00:50:46.760 --> 00:50:53.120
The fact that we are so genetically similar to gorillas, sharing 98% of the same genes,

00:50:53.120 --> 00:50:58.800
means that gorillas can catch our diseases.

00:50:58.800 --> 00:51:04.240
That's why it's so important that human visitors to the gorillas must be in good health and

00:51:04.240 --> 00:51:09.720
that they keep to a distance of seven meters.

00:51:09.720 --> 00:51:15.760
Visits are also limited to just one hour to reduce our impact on the animals.

00:51:15.760 --> 00:51:20.000
People from the Mountain Gorilla Veterinary Project and the National Park monitor the

00:51:20.000 --> 00:51:25.440
health of the gorillas in the Varunga Mountains every day.

00:51:25.440 --> 00:51:30.920
Gorillas can be prone to respiratory illnesses, but the vets only intervene if an illness

00:51:30.920 --> 00:51:38.240
or injury is potentially life-threatening.

00:51:38.240 --> 00:51:44.320
Treatment can be disruptive to the group and dangerous for both the people and the gorillas.

00:51:44.320 --> 00:51:54.920
Prevention is better than cure, and eliminating disease here in both humans and animals is

00:51:54.920 --> 00:51:57.600
of the greatest importance.

00:51:57.600 --> 00:52:02.320
The fact that people live and work so close to the edge of the park increases the risk

00:52:02.320 --> 00:52:10.080
of the spread of disease from humans to gorillas and vice versa.

00:52:10.080 --> 00:52:15.800
To improve the health of the human community, organizations like the Dian Fossey Gorilla

00:52:15.800 --> 00:52:21.360
Fund International are supporting clinics surrounding the park, providing medicine and

00:52:21.360 --> 00:52:26.280
health education.

00:52:26.280 --> 00:52:31.720
The hope is that the health of the people and the gorillas will improve as a result,

00:52:31.720 --> 00:52:36.200
and that the local community will feel that they themselves are benefiting from gorilla

00:52:36.200 --> 00:52:46.800
conservation.

00:52:46.800 --> 00:52:52.080
Protecting the gorillas' habitat, preventing poaching and the transmission of disease,

00:52:52.080 --> 00:52:56.880
providing them with veterinary care and giving them a real earning power through tourism

00:52:56.880 --> 00:53:02.640
has resulted in a significant increase in the gorillas' numbers.

00:53:02.640 --> 00:53:07.440
But this success would not have been possible without political stability and the government's

00:53:07.440 --> 00:53:11.360
commitment to ensure their survival.

00:53:11.360 --> 00:53:15.680
Rwanda is proud of its growing population of mountain gorillas.

00:53:15.680 --> 00:53:22.560
Recently, the president of Rwanda, Paul Gagami, hosted a gorilla naming ceremony to celebrate

00:53:22.560 --> 00:53:28.680
the birth of 27 new baby gorillas in the park.

00:53:28.680 --> 00:53:34.200
We in Rwanda are the custodians of the gorillas plus our neighbors, but we need to involve

00:53:34.200 --> 00:53:37.280
as many people as possible.

00:53:37.280 --> 00:53:44.880
We want to tell the world that these are the only remaining gorillas in the world, so every

00:53:44.880 --> 00:53:48.280
person on this planet has got to have a stake.

00:53:48.280 --> 00:53:53.720
It's a fragile success, but I know that if everybody is committed, then we will really

00:53:53.720 --> 00:53:58.360
make it a reality for all the generations to come.

00:53:58.360 --> 00:54:04.920
So the future of mountain gorillas is looking hopeful, but the prospect for eastern and

00:54:04.920 --> 00:54:08.080
western lowland gorillas is much more bleak.

00:54:08.080 --> 00:54:13.080
There are fewer than 90,000 of them left in the world, and their numbers are declining

00:54:13.080 --> 00:54:22.200
rapidly as a result of deforestation, hunting, and diseases such as Ebola.

00:54:22.200 --> 00:54:26.280
Little of their habitat is protected, and the areas in which they live are affected

00:54:26.280 --> 00:54:33.320
by civil war, lawlessness, and extreme poverty.

00:54:33.320 --> 00:54:36.360
Gorillas are not the only endangered great ape.

00:54:36.360 --> 00:54:42.480
Orangutans, chimpanzees, and bonobos all face similar threats, and if we don't act now,

00:54:42.480 --> 00:54:45.680
they will become extinct in our children's lifetime.

00:54:45.680 --> 00:54:52.200
We are going to need serious commitment at the highest levels if we are to save the world's

00:54:52.200 --> 00:54:59.080
remaining great apes.

00:54:59.080 --> 00:55:02.480
The first big step has already been taken.

00:55:02.480 --> 00:55:09.120
In September 2005, an international meeting was brokered by the United Nations in the

00:55:09.120 --> 00:55:12.160
Democratic Republic of Congo.

00:55:12.160 --> 00:55:17.560
Countries and organizations were encouraged to sign up to the Kinshasa Agreement to save

00:55:17.560 --> 00:55:20.440
great apes from extinction.

00:55:20.440 --> 00:55:24.600
And Redmond believes this is a positive step forward.

00:55:24.600 --> 00:55:30.240
All those individual efforts from small organizations and big organizations have now been knitted

00:55:30.240 --> 00:55:32.400
together into a global strategy.

00:55:32.400 --> 00:55:36.440
Instead of individual little heroic efforts going on here and here, but winning the odd

00:55:36.440 --> 00:55:41.960
battle but losing the war, we now have a chance to actually strategically take on the threats

00:55:41.960 --> 00:55:45.460
to the great apes, and that's very exciting.

00:55:45.460 --> 00:55:50.600
The declaration requires its signatories to reduce practices that are wiping out great

00:55:50.600 --> 00:55:55.480
apes and enforce laws to protect them.

00:55:55.480 --> 00:56:00.160
It is evident from this week's proceedings that strong international cooperation will

00:56:00.160 --> 00:56:05.920
be forthcoming to assure the long-term survival of the great apes.

00:56:05.920 --> 00:56:10.480
The political will is encouraging, but the goal of securing the future of the great apes

00:56:10.480 --> 00:56:19.680
in the wild by 2015 will only be realized if all parties fulfill their promises.

00:56:19.680 --> 00:56:25.360
It was after returning from the historic meeting in Kinshasa that Ian showed us some recent

00:56:25.360 --> 00:56:31.360
footage of the mountain gorillas that we'd first met in Rwanda over 25 years ago.

00:56:31.360 --> 00:56:34.360
Oh dear.

00:56:34.360 --> 00:56:37.360
Oh, trouble.

00:56:37.360 --> 00:56:38.360
Spawning.

00:56:38.360 --> 00:56:44.320
I would be with, with doing all the submissive gestures I could think of.

00:56:44.320 --> 00:56:45.320
Grunting like mad.

00:56:45.320 --> 00:56:46.320
Yes.

00:56:46.320 --> 00:56:47.320
It looks like Titus.

00:56:47.320 --> 00:56:48.320
Is that Titus?

00:56:48.320 --> 00:56:49.320
Yeah.

00:56:49.320 --> 00:56:50.320
Is it?

00:56:50.320 --> 00:56:57.480
The last time we'd seen Titus, he was a lively, inquisitive youngster, but we were in for

00:56:57.480 --> 00:57:01.080
more of a shock when we saw what had become of young Pablo.

00:57:01.080 --> 00:57:07.640
Here he is sitting on me in 1978.

00:57:07.640 --> 00:57:13.440
And here he is today, a 200 kilo dominant silverback, king of his group.

00:57:13.440 --> 00:57:15.280
So that's Pablo.

00:57:15.280 --> 00:57:16.280
That's Pablo, is it?

00:57:16.280 --> 00:57:19.280
The one that was sitting on my feet.

00:57:19.280 --> 00:57:20.280
Yes.

00:57:20.280 --> 00:57:24.640
Good job he's not sitting on your feet now, is he?

00:57:24.640 --> 00:57:31.640
And Pablo is the leader now of the biggest group of gorillas on record with 59 individuals.

00:57:31.640 --> 00:57:32.640
59?

00:57:32.640 --> 00:57:33.640
Yes.

00:57:33.640 --> 00:57:39.520
I mean they're rewriting the gorilla sociology books.

00:57:39.520 --> 00:57:45.160
It's great news that Pablo's group has grown to a record breaking size.

00:57:45.160 --> 00:57:51.720
In fact, the total number of mountain gorillas in the Varungas has increased by 120 individuals

00:57:51.720 --> 00:57:58.000
in the last 25 years to a high at the last count of 380.

00:57:58.000 --> 00:58:03.760
It is now our responsibility to learn lessons from the mountain gorilla conservation story

00:58:03.760 --> 00:58:08.360
and help all the other great apes that are still so seriously endangered.

00:58:08.360 --> 00:58:20.720
There's a long way to go, but we too can pay our part in helping to save the great apes.

00:58:20.720 --> 00:58:28.280
Thanks to individuals like these, to conservation organisations, to communities, to governments,

00:58:28.280 --> 00:58:33.240
there really is hope for the mountain gorilla and the other great apes.

00:58:33.240 --> 00:58:39.960
But we continue to destroy so much of the natural world that danger is ever present.

00:58:39.960 --> 00:58:50.960
The time really has come to show our closest cousins that we do care.
